task: json-fast-read (the operator's call, 2026-09-07: "let there be a choice")
agent: okay-42
started: 2026-09-07
branch: feature/json-fast-read

why: Json.read is the lossless road -- scanner, CST with every trivia
  token, projection, Schema fold -- and reads 10.3us where circe reads
  0.623 (section on codecs, "our JSON read is 16x slower"). It buys
  byte-for-byte losslessness, damage-as-data and a HALF-ARRIVED
  document that still decodes. Most callers decoding a complete,
  well-formed document want none of that and pay for all of it.

what: a SECOND door, named for what it gives up -- Json.readStrict[A]
  (name settled in the lane) -- that goes chars -> Schema straight,
  the way Cbor.read does: the strict recursive descent of
  JsonValue.Parser (index, skipWs, str, num) driving Cbor.get's Schema
  walk, so no Json tree is built either. Products by field name with
  the same default/None-if-optional rules as the lossless decoder,
  sums in the same one-entry-object shape Json.encode writes, options
  as null, lists/vectors, iso, bytes and chars as the lossless road
  reads them. Refuses -- Left -- anything it is not sure of, exactly
  as JsonValue.parse answers None.

the law: for every a, readStrict(encode(a)) == decode(parse(encode(a)))
  -- the same answer as the lossless road on well-formed input, checked
  on the codec test corpus; and on a truncated or damaged document the
  strict road answers Left where the lossless one still projects --
  that is the trade, stated as a test, not a footnote.

measure: CodecBenchmark, a textToOrderStrict lane against
  textToOrderCirce (0.623) and the lossless textToOrder (10.3); bytes
  and time. The doc's price-list table gains a row and the sentence
  that tells a caller which door is theirs.
